Q: MySQL vs MariaDB — what’s the difference?

Both are relational databases with very similar SQL and client protocols.
MariaDB started as a **fork of MySQL** (by original MySQL creators) after Oracle acquired MySQL.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ MySQL
- Owner: Oracle Corporation (originally MySQL AB)
- License: dual — GPL community + commercial options
- Positioning: industry-default / enterprise-supported
- Evolution: more conservative feature pace
- Storage: InnoDB is the main workhorse (plus others)
- Ecosystem: huge hosting, cloud, tool, and hiring support

✅ MariaDB
- Owner/community: MariaDB Foundation / MariaDB plc ecosystem
- License: strongly open-source oriented (GPL)
- Started as MySQL fork; same roots, diverged over time
- Often adds features/engines faster (e.g. Aria, ColumnStore historically)
- Aims for MySQL compatibility, but **not 100% drop-in forever**

────────────────────────────────────────
⚖️ Summary

| Feature           | MySQL                         | MariaDB                              |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Owner             | Oracle                        | MariaDB Foundation / company         |
| Origin            | Original product              | Fork of MySQL                        |
| License story     | Community + commercial        | Fully OSS-focused (GPL)              |
| Feature pace      | More conservative             | Often more aggressive                |
| Engines           | Mainly InnoDB focus           | Extra engines (Aria, ColumnStore…)   |
| Compatibility     | Standard reference            | Mostly MySQL-compatible              |
| Cloud commonality | Very common (RDS MySQL, etc.) | Also common (incl. MariaDB services) |

────────────────────────────────────────
⚠️ Say carefully in interviews (don’t oversell)

1) “MariaDB is always faster” → ❌ not universally true. Depends on version, workload, tuning.
2) “100% drop-in replacement” → ❌ **mostly** compatible; newer versions diverged (JSON, auth, optimizer, system tables, some SQL).
3) Always test migrations (app SQL, replication, backup tools, connectors).

Practical Spring Boot note:
Same JDBC style (`mysql` vs `mariadb` driver/url). Prefer the driver matching your server.
Example URLs:
- jdbc:mysql://host:3306/db
- jdbc:mariadb://host:3306/db

────────────────────────────────────────
When to choose what

Choose MySQL if:
- company standard / Oracle ecosystem / AWS RDS MySQL already in place
- you want maximum “everyone knows this” compatibility

Choose MariaDB if:
- team wants OSS-governed fork / specific MariaDB features/engines
- infra already standardized on MariaDB

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer

“MariaDB is a fork of MySQL created after Oracle’s acquisition. They share similar SQL and are largely compatible, but have diverged.
MySQL is Oracle-backed and very widely used; MariaDB is more community/OSS-driven and often ships extra engines/features.
I wouldn’t claim one is always faster — I’d choose based on company standard, compatibility needs, and prove with benchmarks/migrations.”
